4.103.m:
Tell me, O Lord, in which direction is water auspicious and in which inauspicious? // Which tree brings fortune, and in which direction should it be situated? / What is the measurement of the houses and the courtyards, O Lord of the gods? // In which direction is a flower garden auspicious, and in which that of a tree? / What is the measure of the ramparts and of the moats, O Lord of the gods? // What is the proportion of the doors, the houses, and the ramparts? / The wood of which tree is praised in an encampment, O Lord, // or which are inauspicious? You ought to tell me everything.
The Lord said:
In a dwelling, the coconut tree bestows wealth upon householders; / if planted in the northeast of the encampment, or in the east, the tree bestows sons. // It is worthy of auspiciousness everywhere, a captivating king of trees. / The mango tree in the east likewise brings prosperity to men, // and it brings good fortune everywhere; listen, O celestial architect! / The bilva, the jackfruit, the citron, and the jujube: // in the east they grant offspring, and in the south they give wealth; / everywhere they grant prosperity, by which indeed the householder flourishes. // The jambu tree, pomegranate, plantain, and āmrātaka: / in the east they give relatives, and in the south they grant friends; // everywhere they grant fortune, bestowing wealth, sons, and good luck. / The betel-nut palm brings joy in the south and west; // in the northeast it brings happiness, and everywhere [it is auspicious]—hear! / The campaka is pure everywhere and brings good fortune on earth. // The bottle gourd, the pumpkin, the āyāmbu, along with the kiṃśuka, / the date palm, and the cucumber bring auspiciousness in the encampment; // spinach, bitter gourd, and eggplant bring good fortune; / indeed, every climbing plant's fruit brings good fortune everywhere, without doubt. // The praised ones have been declared, O architect; now listen to the forbidden ones. / A wild tree is forbidden both in an encampment and in a city. // The banyan tree is forbidden in an encampment, for there is constant fear of thieves;
